WEBVTT

00:00:07.552 --> 00:00:14.250
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> The Pitt Rivers Museum in Oxford was founded in 1884, by Augustus Pitt Rivers,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:18.854
a British army officer, ethnologist, and archaeologist.

00:00:20.034 --> 00:00:24.010
The displays were installed by its first curator, Henry Balfour,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.320
and catalogued by the anthropologist, Beatrice Blackwood.

00:00:28.894 --> 00:00:33.615
And today, this eccentric and quirky space is like a trip back in time.

00:00:34.421 --> 00:00:39.124
It’s crammed with around 55,000 objects on display,

00:00:39.484 --> 00:00:43.991
ranging from prehistoric handaxes and a witch in a bottle,

00:00:44.545 --> 00:00:48.695
to a mask with movable eyes, and a leaf-shaped dagger.

00:00:50.192 --> 00:00:56.992
And behind the scenes, the museum, looks after another 550,000 items,

00:00:57.245 --> 00:01:03.088
cataloguing, conserving, and studying the many different historic items it houses.

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:09.682
The Pitt Rivers has often been described as a ‘museum, of a museum’,

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:15.717
feeling at once old and new, traditional and contemporary.

00:01:17.084 --> 00:01:19.813
The mix of styles is extremely popular,

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:25.245
with visitor numbers climbing to over 500,000 in the last few years.

00:01:27.666 --> 00:01:35.147
A sensitive modernization programme has included the installation of 170 metres of new displays

00:01:36.069 --> 00:01:42.045
– that’s almost the length of two Big Bens – the addition of 1,500 new exhibits,

00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:45.680
and the digitization of 10,000 photographs.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.420
They’ve even lit up the whole space with tons of energy-efficient LEDs,

00:01:51.786 --> 00:01:54.346
transforming many of their displays.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.978
All of this shows that the Pitt Rivers is looking to the future,

00:01:59.931 --> 00:02:03.621
but to do so, it also needs to face up to its past.

00:02:05.723 --> 00:02:11.357
Like most 19th-century European museums, the Pitt Rivers has its roots in colonialism.

00:02:12.918 --> 00:02:18.863
When it opened in 1884, the British Empire stretched from North America to New Zealand

00:02:19.323 --> 00:02:23.004
and included large parts of Africa and Asia.

00:02:23.645 --> 00:02:34.264
Other European countries, such as Belgium, France, and the Netherlands had similar colonies.

00:02:40.963 --> 00:02:45.817
At the time, it was common for these colonial powers to send people across the world,

00:02:46.464 --> 00:02:52.025
with the army, to work, for study, or, sometimes, just for fun.

00:02:54.235 --> 00:02:59.695
As they travelled, some even chronicled their experiences with the emerging technology of film.

00:03:02.437 --> 00:03:04.992
Looking through the Pitt Rivers’ film archive today,

00:03:04.992 --> 00:03:08.246
you get a sense of what it was like for these European travellers,

00:03:08.765 --> 00:03:13.630
encountering people and places they knew little or nothing about.

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:21.700
They had suddenly discovered new and different landscapes and creatures,

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:28.953
witnessed rituals and religious ceremonies, and been fascinated by the clothes of both rich

00:03:28.953 --> 00:03:34.356
and poor, as well as by the local craftsmanship they observed.

00:03:37.478 --> 00:03:39.931
But you can also see the colonial mindset,

00:03:40.377 --> 00:03:44.518
where what they saw as foreign and exotic was to be captured, relocated,

00:03:44.518 --> 00:03:46.711
and displayed for those back home in Europe.

00:03:48.657 --> 00:03:50.561
They didn’t just take photos or film.

00:03:50.961 --> 00:03:55.935
Before long, items of historical interest were being sent to Europe from all over the world.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:06.912
Much of the Pitt Rivers’ collection was ‘acquired’ during the heyday of the British Empire

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:10.120
and the museum often reflected these imperial values.

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:18.072
When object labels were written, for example, they used what is now considered derogatory

00:04:18.072 --> 00:04:19.452
and shocking language.

00:04:20.005 --> 00:04:24.888
When challenged on this in the 1980s, the museum, just painted the offending words out.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:32.460
But things are changing. And to make this change, the Pitt Rivers is inviting the world in.

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:40.519
Now, other voices are pushing the museum, to reassess its own history.

00:04:42.513 --> 00:04:46.538
Some of these are from places where objects in the collection originated.

00:04:47.557 --> 00:04:53.971
Representatives from Haida Gwaii, a group of islands between Canada and Alaska,

00:04:54.511 --> 00:05:01.096
have been in conversation with both the Pitt Rivers in Oxford and the British Museum in London

00:05:01.095 --> 00:05:06.709
to improve relationships between the Haida people and the museums holding their treasures.

00:05:07.383 --> 00:05:13.612
For over a century, Haida artefacts have formed an important part of the Pitt Rivers collection.

00:05:14.405 --> 00:05:19.245
This includes this Haida war helmet, with its carved and painted octopus,

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:24.955
which the Haida believe was worn by prominent chiefs during battle or ceremonial war dances.

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:31.862
And, of course, there’s the Haida totem pole, which has become a symbol of the museum.

00:05:32.195 --> 00:05:36.403
It’s over 11 metres tall, the largest object in the collection,

00:05:36.403 --> 00:05:41.053
and shows seated figures and animals such as a bear, a frog, and a raven.

00:05:44.478 --> 00:05:46.555
These totems serve many purposes,

00:05:46.555 --> 00:05:51.235
such as commemorating important events or telling the stories of the local people.

00:05:54.468 --> 00:05:58.423
All of these items still have huge cultural significance to the local people,

00:05:59.671 --> 00:06:04.405
something Haida artists Jaalen and Gwaii Edenshaw felt immediately

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.978
when they first visited the Pitt Rivers in 2009.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.599
<v><b>Jaalen:</b></v> You always feel two ways when you’re in a museum, like that,

00:06:11.624 --> 00:06:17.521
‘cos it’s so awesome to be in the presence of all this old Haida things, but at the same time,

00:06:17.521 --> 00:06:21.786
you, you know, you feel a sadness because all those things were, ah,

00:06:21.787 --> 00:06:26.886
removed from our village and, and by that removing has lost its purpose in our community.

00:06:27.625 --> 00:06:35.951
<v><b>Gwaii:</b></v> I didn’t expect it actually, but when I walked into the room with the chief’s headdresses,

00:06:36.598 --> 00:06:42.660
it was a very emotional experience for me and, er, it caught me off guard.

00:06:43.394 --> 00:06:49.872
You know, those pieces felt like they were…lost.

00:06:53.053 --> 00:06:57.582
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v>  The importance of these items isn’t limited to the objects themselves;

00:06:58.249 --> 00:07:01.489
they tell us a huge amount about the cultures they come from.

00:07:03.244 --> 00:07:09.202
Take ‘The Great Box’, for example, part of the Pitt Rivers collection since 1874.

00:07:09.689 --> 00:07:11.872
We don’t know exactly what it was used for,

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:16.895
but we do know it is a masterpiece of Haida Nation craftsmanship.

00:07:18.239 --> 00:07:22.414
That’s why Gwaii and Jaalen decided to create a replica.

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:25.900
The brothers spent 28 days at the museum,

00:07:25.925 --> 00:07:29.497
studying the box and re-learning the skills of their ancestors.

00:07:30.797 --> 00:07:35.797
Working 12-hour days, they eventually carved a replica and took the new box

00:07:35.798 --> 00:07:37.789
– and new skills – back home.

00:07:40.003 --> 00:07:45.764
This is just one example of the many projects to welcome more voices to the debate.

00:07:46.317 --> 00:07:50.200
The Pitt Rivers has run workshops, seminars, lectures,

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.138
and meetings to ensure this difficult history – and the hurt it has caused

00:07:55.138 --> 00:08:00.019
– is heard and understood. Of course, this doesn’t right those wrongs,

00:08:00.019 --> 00:08:07.279
but by shining a light on its history, the Pitt Rivers is showing that the past belongs to everyone.